```java
class SharedQueue {
    private final Deque<Task> items = new ArrayDeque<>();

    boolean hasNext() {
        return !items.isEmpty();
    }

    Task next() {
        return items.removeFirst();   // throws if empty!
    }
}

// caller:
if (queue.hasNext()) {
    Task t = queue.next();   // another thread can drain the queue in between
}
```

The caller's `if (hasNext())` and `next()` are two separate calls, not one atomic step. If a second thread calls `next()` on the last remaining item between this thread's check and its own `next()` call, `removeFirst()` throws `NoSuchElementException` on an empty deque — the exact gap between check and act.

```java
class Cache {
    private Map<String, Data> data;

    Map<String, Data> get() {
        if (data == null) {
            data = loadFromDisk();   // two threads can both pass the null check
        }
        return data;
    }
}
```

Two threads calling `get()` for the first time can both see `data == null`, both call `loadFromDisk()`, and both assign the result — one construction is wasted, and worse, if `loadFromDisk()` has side effects (like acquiring a resource), those side effects happen twice. The read-modify-write here is the check-then-construct-then-assign sequence, not a single field access.
